---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Privacy: Your Image Never Leaves | Local Cutout';
const description =
  'Local Cutout processes images in your browser, uses no accounts or cookies, and never uploads your photos.';
---

<BaseLayout {title} {description} path="/privacy/">
  <article class="shell prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1>Your image never leaves your device</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Local Cutout has no image server, account system, database, cookies, or analytics.
    </p>

    <h2>What the tool processes</h2>
    <p>
      The image you choose is decoded, resized, processed, previewed, and exported in memory inside
      your current browser tab. Starting over releases the temporary image URL and working pixels.
    </p>

    <h2>What the network downloads</h2>
    <p>
      The site downloads its own HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and font. Its version-pinned ONNX WebAssembly
      runtime is delivered by jsDelivr. On your first cutout, it also downloads the pinned MODNet
      model files through this site's restricted Cloudflare route from Hugging Face. These files
      describe the processing model; they do not contain or receive your image. If you choose Improve
      accuracy on a WebGPU device, the browser downloads an additional BiRefNet Lite model of about
      100 MB through the same route. Your pixels are still processed only inside the browser worker.
    </p>

    <h2>What we do not collect</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No original or processed images</li>
      <li>No names, email addresses, or accounts</li>
      <li>No cookies or cross-site tracking</li>
      <li>No usage analytics</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to verify the claim</h2>
    <p>
      Open your browser’s developer tools, select the Network panel, process an image, and inspect the
      requests. You will see model and runtime downloads, but no request body containing image data.
    </p>

    <p><a class="button" href="/">Return to the tool</a></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
